"This is the sort-then-sweep template. Adjacent-pair problems — gaps between consecutive items, merge-overlapping-intervals, max-difference-in-sorted-array — all have this shape:

```
const sorted = [...dates].sort();
const gaps = [];
for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {            // start at 1 so i-1 is valid
  const days = (Date.parse(sorted[i]) - Date.parse(sorted[i - 1])) / 86400000;
  if (days > maxDays) gaps.push(`${sorted[i-1]} → ${sorted[i]}: ${days} days`);
}
return gaps;
```

"Four load-bearing pieces. (1) `[...dates]` clones because `.sort()` mutates — protect the caller. (2) `.sort()` defaults to lexicographic, which is chronological for ISO `YYYY-MM-DD` because every component is fixed-width big-endian. (3) `i = 1` is the off-by-one — every iteration reads `sorted[i-1]` AND `sorted[i]`, so the earliest valid i is 1. Starting at 0 would index `sorted[-1] = undefined`. (4) `(Date.parse(b) - Date.parse(a)) / 86400000` converts millisecond difference to whole days; `86400000` is `1000 * 60 * 60 * 24` — milliseconds in a day."

